From: mklab <noreply@example.invalid>
Subject: [PATCH 38/38] amd64 knew only one kind of dictionary, so an embedded one panicked

arch/amd64/openbios.c had only the MULTIBOOT dictionary path, unconditionally.
Fed an embedded image it parsed a ready-to-run dictionary as though it were a
dictionary FILE and never set `last`, so findword() found nothing:

  forth started.
  initializing memory...done
  fword: 'is-noname-cfunc' is not in the dictionary -- nothing was executed
  panic: no dictionary entry point.

...which reads as a dictionary bug rather than a missing branch, because it
happens AFTER long mode and after Forth starts.

This ports arch/x86's `if (sys_info.dict_last)` arm: a builtin image is used in
place, with `last` taken from the image and dict_end read as an OFFSET (both
builtin.c files set it from FORTH_DICTIONARY_END, a constant in the generated
static-dict.h), while the multiboot arm keeps parsing into intdict. arch/x86's
comment on that second arm reads "as arch/amd64 does" -- the code already
recorded that this was the only path here.

With this, a coreboot ROM carrying openbios-builtin.elf32 reaches the prompt on
amd64 and `-1 u.` prints ffffffffffffffff: OpenBIOS in long mode as a coreboot
payload, on the arch that used to build zero objects.

Arch-tested: amd64

diff --git a/arch/amd64/openbios.c b/arch/amd64/openbios.c
--- a/arch/amd64/openbios.c
+++ b/arch/amd64/openbios.c
@@ -195,17 +195,48 @@
 
         collect_sys_info(&sys_info);
 
-	dict = (unsigned char *)intdict;   /* Spike 1 drift: cast, as arch/x86 does */
-	dictlimit = DICTIONARY_SIZE;
+	/* TWO KINDS OF DICTIONARY, AND amd64 KNEW ONLY ONE.
+	 *
+	 * A BUILTIN image (IMAGE_ELF_EMBEDDED -- which is what a coreboot
+	 * payload has to be, since coreboot cannot hand a payload a separate
+	 * module the way -kernel/-initrd does) carries a dictionary that is
+	 * ALREADY relocated and ready to run: it is used in place, and `last`,
+	 * the pointer to its final word, comes from the image. Note dict_end is
+	 * an OFFSET here, not an address -- arch/{x86,amd64}/builtin.c sets it
+	 * from FORTH_DICTIONARY_END, a constant in the generated static-dict.h.
+	 *
+	 * A MULTIBOOT module instead carries a dictionary FILE -- header and
+	 * relocation table and all -- which has to be parsed into intdict.
+	 *
+	 * amd64 had only the second arm, unconditionally, because nothing had
+	 * ever built an embedded image for it. Fed one, it parsed a ready-to-run
+	 * dictionary as though it were a file and never set `last`, so
+	 * findword() found nothing and the boot died in "panic: no dictionary
+	 * entry point" -- AFTER reaching long mode and printing "forth started",
+	 * which is what made it read as a dictionary bug rather than a missing
+	 * branch. arch/x86's comment on its own multiboot arm says "as
+	 * arch/amd64 does": the code already recorded that this was the only
+	 * path here. */
+	if (sys_info.dict_last) {
+		/* builtin image: an embedded, ready-to-run dictionary */
+		dict = (unsigned char *)sys_info.dict_start;
+		dicthead = (cell)sys_info.dict_end;
+		last = sys_info.dict_last;
+		dictlimit = sys_info.dict_limit;
+	} else {
+		dict = (unsigned char *)intdict;   /* Spike 1 drift: cast, as arch/x86 does */
+		dictlimit = DICTIONARY_SIZE;
 
-	/* Spike 1: POINTER SUBTRACTION, not byte subtraction. dict_start and
-	 * dict_end are `unsigned long *`, so the difference is in ELEMENTS --
-	 * a quarter of the byte count on x86 and an EIGHTH of it here. The
-	 * dictionary then checksums over one eighth of itself and fails.
-	 * arch/x86 casts both sides to char* first; this did not. */
-	load_dictionary((char *)sys_info.dict_start,
-			(char *)sys_info.dict_end
-			- (char *)sys_info.dict_start);
+		/* Spike 1: POINTER SUBTRACTION, not byte subtraction. dict_start
+		 * and dict_end are `unsigned long *`, so the difference is in
+		 * ELEMENTS -- a quarter of the byte count on x86 and an EIGHTH of
+		 * it here. The dictionary then checksums over one eighth of itself
+		 * and fails. arch/x86 casts both sides to char* first; this did
+		 * not. */
+		load_dictionary((char *)sys_info.dict_start,
+				(char *)sys_info.dict_end
+				- (char *)sys_info.dict_start);
+	}
 	forth_init();
 
 	relocate(&sys_info);
